  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nConfidence remains high that measurable precipitation will \nremain limited through Sunday as a ridge of high pressure \ndominates the Ohio River Valley. Persistent southwest flow aloft\nwill support temperatures around 10 degrees above average \nthroughout the week. The potential for frost for freeze \nconditions remain very low across the remaining counties where \nthe growing season is still ongoing. However, with the November \n1st cutoff approaching, the likelihood of issuing additional \nfrost/freeze product is considered very low (less than 10%). \n\nA cold front is expected to move across the region late tonight\ninto early Tuesday morning. Fog may develop along or in the\nvicinity of the frontal boundary, primarily south of Pittsburgh\nnear I-70 corridor. \n\nLong-range models suggest a potential disturbance developing\nover the southern Great Plains this weekend and track east or\nnortheast. However, significant run-to-run variability in the\nprojected track continues to contribute to considerable\nuncertainty in rainfall amounts across the region.",
